# BOHEMIA

A Spotify-inspired music player built with **pure HTML, CSS, and JavaScript**, with no frameworks or libraries other than Font Awesome for icons.

🔗 **Demo:** [LeoGB1409.github.io/bohemia](https://github.com/LeoGB1409/Bohemia)

![BOHEMIA em ação](./Preview.png)



> Educational, non-profit project with no affiliation with Spotify.

## Features

### Player

- Click an artist or album to play a song
- Play/pause, previous, and next controls (the previous button restarts the track if more than 3 seconds have already passed, just like Spotify)
- Automatically plays the next song when the current track ends
- Loop button to repeat the current track
- Progress bar showing current and total time, with seeking by dragging
- Volume control and mute button
- Keyboard shortcut: `Space` to play/pause
- Browser tab title updates according to the currently playing song

### Library

- **Liked Songs:** like a song using the player's heart button and access all liked songs in a dedicated section
- **Playlists:** create playlists through a modal, select songs, open playlists, and delete them
- Likes and playlists are stored in the browser using `localStorage` and persist after reloading the page

### Interface

- Real-time search by artist, album, or song, ignoring accents and letter casing
- Greeting changes according to the time of day (Good morning, Good afternoon, Good evening)
- Responsive layout: on mobile, the sidebar becomes an off-canvas menu opened with the ☰ button

## Technologies

| Technology | Usage |
|---|---|
| **HTML5** | Semantic structure |
| **CSS3** | Grid, Flexbox, media queries, CSS variables, and transitions |
| **JavaScript (ES6+)** | DOM manipulation, event delegation, `Audio` API, and `localStorage` |
| **Font Awesome** | Icons |

## Running Locally

```bash
git clone https://github.com/SEU-USUARIO/bohemia.git
cd bohemia
```

Open the folder in VS Code and use the **Live Server** extension, or run a simple local server:

```bash
python -m http.server 5500
```

Then open `http://localhost:5500` in your browser.

Opening `index.html` directly may prevent audio from working in some browsers, which is why a local server is recommended.

## Project Structure

```text
bohemia/
├── index.html
├── style.css
├── scripts.js
├── img/        # Album covers and artist photos
├── audio/      # Audio tracks
└── docs/       # GIFs and README screenshots
```

## Adding a New Song

The music data is stored in two arrays inside `scripts.js`.

To add a new item, simply add an object containing the audio path:

```javascript
{
  name: 'Artist',
  img: './img/artist.jpg',
  audio: './audio/artist.mp3',
  track: 'Song Name'
}
```

Cards, search results, likes, and playlists are generated from this data, so there is no need to modify the HTML manually.

## What I Learned

- **Event delegation:** using a single listener for each card grid instead of one listener per card, with `closest()` to identify the clicked card.
- **Single source of truth:** the UI (play/pause icon and active card state) is updated through the `Audio` element's `play` and `pause` events rather than directly from click handlers.
- **Audio events:** working with `loadedmetadata`, `durationchange`, `timeupdate`, and `ended`, as well as understanding why duration can sometimes return `NaN` or `Infinity` for certain audio files.
- **Persistence:** using `localStorage` with `try/catch` to handle corrupted data safely.
- **Basic security:** user-entered text is inserted into the page using `textContent`, never `innerHTML`.
- **Responsive design:** implementing an off-canvas sidebar using `transform` and media queries.

